# Target C-II — bridge suppression forces transverse contacts in the long-side branch

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `32a1e08d7bfcc6c69aa6e286ca183f573cf9f36c`  
**Status:** proved proof-only composition; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** no-container Type-II full positive-contact core  
**Dependencies:** loaded-hull interval decomposition (§63), C26 no-neutral theorem, bridge-subtracted full-core isoperimetry, long-side straight-event dichotomy  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The preceding long-side straight-event theorem left a two-way alternative: a linear
population of loaded degree-two boundary events, or a linear population of strongly
transverse extra contacts. In the full-core Type-II branch, the first alternative is
not genuinely available. Such a degree-two event is necessarily a repeated
outer-face occurrence of a loaded vertex, hence an outer-bridge event. Full-core
near-disk area saturation bounds the total number of outer bridges by the outer-walk
detour.

The result is a direct one-branch theorem: the current rounded long-side escape forces
linearly many strongly transverse physical unit contacts.

## 1. A loaded internal route occurrence is non-distinguished

Section 63 chooses one distinguished exterior occurrence of each loaded force-hull
vertex and cuts the outer-face boundary walk at those occurrences. The distinguished
occurrences appear in loaded-hull cyclic order, and

\[
W_i:b_i\leadsto b_{i+1}
\]

is the subwalk between consecutive distinguished loaded occurrences.

Let `x` be an internal physical vertex occurrence of one `W_i`. If `x` is loaded, this
occurrence cannot be the distinguished occurrence of `x`: no distinguished loaded
occurrence lies strictly between the consecutive distinguished occurrences of `b_i`
and `b_{i+1}`. Therefore a loaded internal route occurrence is a repeated occurrence
of the same physical vertex on the outer-face walk.

## 2. Degree two makes the repeated event an outer-bridge event

A vertex that occurs more than once on a face-boundary walk of a connected plane graph
is a cutvertex of the corresponding block decomposition. If such a vertex `x` has
full contact degree exactly two, both incident contact edges are bridges: deleting one
of the two edges otherwise leaves an alternate path through the unique other incident
edge, which would reconnect the two sides of `G-x`, contradicting that `x` is a
cutvertex.

For the repeated occurrence under discussion, both incident bridge sides occur on the
unbounded-face walk. A bridge is incident to the same face on both sides, so both
physical bridges are outer bridges.

Let `V_2(beta)` be the number of distinct physical degree-two vertices supporting a
`beta`-straight internal occurrence on a long-side route, as in the straight-event
note, and let `B_o` be the number of outer bridges. C26 makes every such degree-two
vertex loaded. The argument above therefore applies to every vertex counted by
`V_2(beta)`.

Counting bridge incidences gives

\[
2V_2(\beta)\le2B_o,
\]

because each counted vertex is incident to two distinct outer bridges and each bridge
has at most two endpoints. Thus

\[
\boxed{V_2(\beta)\le B_o.}
\tag{BS.1}
\]

This is finite.

## 3. Full-core area saturation bounds the bridge population

For the no-container Type-II full positive-contact core, the existing near-disk stack
squeezes the full bounded-face union to asymptotic area `pi D^2/4`. The
bridge-subtracted isoperimetric theorem therefore gives, if `b/D -> M`,

\[
\limsup {2B_o\over D}\le M-\pi.
\tag{BS.2}
\]

Type II also has `P_F/D->pi`, while

\[
d_{out}=b-P_F.
\]

Hence along any convergent Type-II subsequence with

\[
\delta:=\lim {d_{out}\over D},
\]

we have `M=pi+delta`, and (BS.1)--(BS.2) give

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {2V_2(\beta)\over D}\le\delta.
}
\tag{BS.3}
\]

Thus near-geodesic outer routes cannot hide a linear population of degree-two loaded
repetitions: every such event consumes actual bridge-subtracted perimeter.

## 4. Insert the straight-event tradeoff

Let `E_perp(beta)` be the number of distinct extra contact edges selected from
branching `beta`-straight long-side events. The previous finite event theorem gives

\[
2V_2(\beta)+10E_\perp(\beta)
\ge
L_{>2}-{2d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}.
\tag{BS.4}
\]

Combining with (BS.3), along one convergent Type-II subsequence,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {10E_\perp(\beta)\over D}
\ge
\liminf {L_{>2}\over D}
-\delta\left(1+{2\over1-\cos\beta}\right).
}
\tag{BS.5}
\]

Every selected edge has absolute transverse component greater than

\[
\sin(\pi/3-\beta)
\]

relative to the corresponding long loaded-hull side direction.

No individual KKT coefficient lower bound is used.

## 5. Current low-`t` long-side consequence

At the current proof-only floor `t=t_42`, the long-side alternative gives

\[
{L_{>2}\over D}
\ge0.4363893718701161781\ldots-o(1),
\]

while the low-`t` squeeze gives

\[
\delta\le0.002883975.
\]

Choose

\[
\beta={\pi\over6}.
\]

Then

\[
1-\cos\beta=1-{\sqrt3\over2},
\qquad
\sin(\pi/3-\beta)={1\over2}.
\]

The bad-edge term is

\[
{\delta\over1-\cos\beta}
\le0.02152628245551690578\ldots,
\]

and (BS.5) yields

\[
\boxed{
{E_\perp(\pi/6)\over D}
\ge0.03904528319590823665\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{BS.6}
\]

Every edge in this family has absolute transverse component strictly greater than
`1/2` relative to its long-side tangent direction.

This doubles the unconditional transverse-edge floor from the previous crude
`0.0196668D` alternative and, more importantly, removes the linear loaded-degree-two
escape entirely.

## 6. Updated low-`t` physical normal form

At the current low-`t` floor, every full-core Type-II survivor now satisfies one of
the following two source-level geometric conclusions:

```text
tooth branch:
    >= 0.2127872328 D-o(D) distinct inward unit contacts,
    each with inward component > 0.5011115;

rounded long-side branch:
    >= 0.03904528319 D-o(D) distinct physical unit contacts,
    each with transverse component > 1/2 relative to its long-side direction.
```

The previous third possibility, a macroscopic population of loaded degree-two
near-straight route events, is absorbed by bridge-subtracted isoperimetry.

This does not close C-II. It does remove a genuine branch from the low-`t` endpoint.
The remaining long-side contacts are now forced to penetrate across the nearly
tangential outer-route geometry. The next useful theorem should charge these
transverse contacts to stopped-train phase/interface capacity or use them in a
simultaneous boundary deformation.

## 7. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_long_side_bridge_suppression.py
```

for the Decimal constant checks.
